\begin{proof}
View $f=x^2+y^2-1$ as a polynomial in $x$ over the field $\mathbb{Q}(y)$. If $f$ were reducible in $\mathbb{Q}[x,y]$, then it would be reducible in $\mathbb{Q}(y)[x]$. Since $f$ is quadratic in $x$, reducibility there would imply that its discriminant
\[
\Delta=4(1-y^2)=4(1-y)(1+y)
\]
is a square in $\mathbb{Q}(y)$. It is not a square, because the irreducible factors $1-y$ and $1+y$ each occur with odd exponent. Hence $f$ is irreducible in $\mathbb{Q}(y)[x]$, and therefore in $\mathbb{Q}[x,y]$.\end{proof}
